/* ---- Stage 5a: formatting toolbar + expand toggle on the message editor ----
 *
 * Everything here is dormant by default: AbstractMessageEditor::expandButtonVisible defaults
 * false and the toolbar only exists on screen while isExpanded(), so a host that never opts in
 * renders exactly as it did before this file existed.
 *
 * Deliberately NO "qproperty-maxExpandedHeight" rule: a qproperty- declaration in a shipped
 * stylesheet is re-applied on every repolish (QStyleSheetStyle::setProperties()) and would
 * silently overwrite a host's own EnhancedTextEdit::setMaxExpandedHeight() call. The default
 * lives in C++ as EnhancedTextEdit::DefaultMaxExpandedHeight. A host that wants a different
 * ceiling writes a plain "max-height" on the QTextEdit, which effectiveMaxHeight() already
 * honours in preference to the C++ default. */

uise--MessageEditorToolbar {
padding: 2px 4px;
}

/* An IconTextButton's icon has no sensible default size of its own -- must be set explicitly per
   container, same as editbar.qss/chatimageviewer.qss do for their own button rows.

   Targeted at the two side FRAMES rather than at #expandButton by name, so a host's own icon
   button dropped in with addLeadingWidget()/addTrailingWidget() comes out the same size as the
   expand button it sits next to instead of needing the host to redeclare all of this. The expand
   button is itself a member of #leadingWidgets, so it is still covered.

   The SIDE frames are deliberately sized larger than the toolbar's own buttons below: what a host
   puts there is a composer's primary actions (attach, send), which were 26px in whitemdesktop
   before they moved inside the editor and read as too small at the toolbar's density. The
   formatting toolbar's own buttons are a dense strip of secondary controls and stay compact. */
uise--MessageEditor #leadingWidgets uise--IconTextButton uise--RoundedImage,
uise--MessageEditor #trailingWidgets uise--IconTextButton uise--RoundedImage {
min-width: 26px;
max-width: 26px;
min-height: 26px;
max-height: 26px;
}

uise--MessageEditorToolbar uise--IconTextButton uise--RoundedImage {
min-width: 18px;
max-width: 18px;
min-height: 18px;
max-height: 18px;
}

uise--MessageEditorToolbar uise--IconTextButton,
uise--MessageEditor #leadingWidgets uise--IconTextButton,
uise--MessageEditor #trailingWidgets uise--IconTextButton {
padding: 6px;
margin: 0 1px;
border-radius: 4px;
border: 1px solid transparent;
}

/* No horizontal margin on the LEADING buttons: the 6px padding above already separates them from
   each other and from the composer's edge, and stacking a margin on top of it only pushes the
   group inward without making it any easier to hit. The vertical half is left to the shared rule.
   The trailing group keeps its inset -- see below. */
uise--MessageEditor #leadingWidgets uise--IconTextButton {
margin-left: 0px;
margin-right: 0px;
}

/* ...except on the expand button, whose right margin is not spacing between buttons at all: it is
   the LAST leading item, so that gap separates the whole group from the TEXT AREA. Re-stated here
   because the rule above (2 types + 1 id) would otherwise out-specify #expandButton's own margin
   shorthand further down (1 type + 1 id) and leave the button flush against the text. */
uise--MessageEditor #leadingWidgets #expandButton {
margin-right: 4px;
}

uise--MessageEditor #leadingWidgets {
margin-left: 0px;
}

/* Insets the trailing GROUP from the composer's outer edge, rather than adding per-button margins
   that would also open gaps between adjacent buttons. */
uise--MessageEditor #trailingWidgets {
margin-right: 8px;
}

/* light/reset.qss and dark/reset.qss both sort AFTER this file in the cascade (root-dir files
 * load before theme-dir files, and within a directory QDir::entryInfoList()'s name order is the
 * cascade order -- src/style.cpp:116-125,144-199 -- and 'r' > 'm'). Both carry a bare
 * "QFrame:disabled { background-color; border; color }" rule (light/reset.qss:9,
 * dark/reset.qss). Its specificity (one pseudo-class + one element) beats a two-element plain
 * descendant selector, so a disabled toolbar button -- which is the whole formatting half of the
 * bar outside MessageEditingMode::Wysiwyg -- would inherit reset.qss's border and colour unless
 * this rule carries a pseudo-class of its own. */
uise--MessageEditorToolbar uise--IconTextButton:disabled {
border: 1px solid transparent;
}

uise--MessageEditorToolbar #separator {
min-width: 1px;
max-width: 1px;
margin: 3px 6px;
}

/* The expand button sits to the LEFT of the text edit, sharing its row and bottom-aligned to it
   (see MessageEditor's constructor) -- so it needs a gap on its right to separate it from the
   editor, and a small one below to lift it off the row's bottom edge rather than a top margin,
   which is what it needed back when it had a row of its own underneath the editor. */
uise--MessageEditor #expandButton {
margin: 0 4px 2px 0;
}
